s, those with an average OCS greater than 80% across the six models included I	Luo, Xufei; Li, Zeming; Yang, Zhenhua; et al.	2025	Journal of Medical Internet Research	10.2196/72412	https://www.jmir.org/2025/1/e72412	SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.jmir.org/2025/1/e72412/PDF	False	False			Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.903	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.87	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.888	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.843	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.824	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.732	0.889	OASIS Lab:JAEHTTQ2
Peer review reports	Generate or judge peer-review reports	S6	Screen first: all 3 models	3 of 3	yes	3	Mind the Blind Spots: A Focus-Level Evaluation Framework for LLM Reviews	Peer review underpins scientific progress, but it is increasingly strained by reviewer shortages and growing workloads. Large Language Models (LLMs) can automatically draft reviews now, but determining whether LLM-generated reviews are trustworthy requires systematic evaluation. Researchers have evaluated LLM reviews at either surface-level (e.g., BLEU and ROUGE) or content-level (e.g., specificity and factual accuracy). Yet it remains uncertain whether LLM-generated reviews attend to the same critical facets that human experts weigh—the strengths and weaknesses that ultimately drive an accept-or-reject decision. We introduce a focus-level evaluation framework that operationalizes the focus as a normalized distribution of attention across predefined facets in paper reviews. Based on the framework, we developed an automatic focus-level evaluation pipeline based on two sets of facets: target (e.g., problem, method, and experiment) and aspect (e.g., validity, clarity, and novelty), leveraging 676 paper reviews from OpenReview that consists of 3,657 strengths and weaknesses identified from human experts. The comparison of focus distributions between LLMs and human experts showed that the off-the-shelf LLMs consistently have a more biased focus towards examining technical validity while significantly overlooking novelty assessment when criticizing papers.Dataset: https://figshare.com/s/d5adf26c802527dd0f62	Shin, Hyungyu; Tang, Jingyu; Lee, Yoonjoo; et al.	2025	Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing	10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1805	https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1805/	SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1805	True	False			Peer review reports	0.936	Peer review r