LLMs for risk-of-bias assessment (new)	Assess risk of bias in trials with language models	N30	Screen first: all 3 models	3 of 3	yes	3	Reliability of the risk of bias assessment in randomized controlled trials for nursing: A cross-sectional study.	AIM To evaluate the percentage and reasons for disagreements in the risk of bias (RoB) assessments for randomized controlled trials (RCTs) included in more than one Cochrane review in the field of nursing.   BACKGROUND Disagreement in RoB assessments reduces the credibility of the evidence summarized by systematic reviews (SRs). There is no study that evaluates the reliability of RoB assessments in nursing studies.   DESIGN Secondary data analysis based on research reports.   METHODS RCTs included in more than one review in the nursing have been included. The disagreement of the assessment was analysed, and the possible reasons for disagreements were investigated.   RESULTS Twenty-three RCTs were included in more than one review. The agreement of assessment ranged from 36.84\% for ``selective reporting`` to 91.30\% for ``random sequence generation``. ``Allocation concealment`` showed the optimal agreement (84.21\%). The items ``blinding of participants and personnel``, ``blinding of outcome assessment`` and ``incomplete outcome data`` showed poor agreement, with 50.00\%, 58.82\% and 66.67\%, respectively. Most disagreements came from extracting incomplete or different RCTs' information.   CONCLUSIONS The level of agreement of the assessment between reviews has varied greatly in the field of nursing. More complete and accurate information of RCTs needs to be collected when conducting a SR.	Yao, Yi; Li, Nian; Li, Jieling; et al.	2024		10.1111/ijn.13302		(other folder)	Undermind	OASIS Lab	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1111/ijn.13302	True	False		Not in an ingest folder: found because it is close to the seed and Elicit papers	NEW	0.834	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.783	NEW	0.