Current research has focused on the use of large language models (LLMs) to augment systematic reviews. LLMs are limited by their vulnerability to “hallucinations”; retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) reduces these by limiting the model’s source knowledge to user-provided material. The purpose of this study was to evaluate the accuracy and reliability of a RAG-LLM in quality assessment of observational studies in pediatric orthopedic literature as compared to manual review.
Methods
Previously published systematic reviews of observational studies in pediatric orthopedics containing reported Newcastle-Ottawa Scale (NOS) scores from our group were included. After uploading observational study source files, NotebookLM (Google, Mountain View, CA) evaluated each of the included studies using the NOS scoring sheet. Agreement between scores across all NotebookLM trials was determined using a two-way random, average measures, absolute agreement intraclass correlation coefficient [ICC(2,k)]. Agreement between individual scores generated by each NotebookLM instance (LM1, LM2, LM3, LM4) and ground truth (published manual review score) was calculated using a two-way random, single measures, absolute agreement intraclass correlation coefficient [ICC(2,1)].
Results
