s provide evidence of the potential LLMs may have for medical application and highli	Feng, Hui; Ronzano, Francesco; LaFleur, Jude; et al.	2024		10.1101/2024.05.17.24307411		(other folder)	Undermind	OASIS Lab	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2024/05/17/2024.05.17.24307411.full.pdf	True	False		Not in an ingest folder: found because it is close to the seed and Elicit papers	NEW	0.881	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.87	NEW	0.869	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.856	NEW	0.744	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.744	0.855	OASIS Lab:SZBCXRPT
Automating systematic review screening and extraction (new)	Screen, extract or classify review content with ML and AI	N21	Screen: 2 of 3 models or DNA-VL-STEER-2B	3 of 3	yes	2	Development of meta-prompts for Large Language Models to screen titles and abstracts for diagnostic test accuracy reviews	Systematic reviews (SRs) are a critical component of evidence-based medicine, but the process of screening titles and abstracts is time-consuming. This study aimed to develop and externally validate a method using large language models to classify abstracts for diagnostic test accuracy (DTA) systematic reviews, thereby reducing the human workload. We used a previously collected dataset for developing DTA abstract classifiers and applied prompt engineering. We developed an optimized meta-prompt for Generative Pre-trained Transformer (GPT)-3.5-turbo and GPT-4 to classify abstracts. In the external validation dataset 1, the prompt with GPT-3.5 turbo showed a sensitivity of 0.988, and a specificity of 0.298. GPT-4 showed a sensitivity of 0.982, and a specificity of 0.677. In the external validation dataset 2, GPT-3.5 turbo showed a sensitivity of 0.919, and a specificity of 0.434. GPT-4 showed a sensitivity of 0.806, and a specificity of 0.740. If we included eligible studies from among the references of the identified studies, GPT-3.5 turbo had no critical misses, while GPT-4 had some misses. Our study indicates that GPT-3.5 turbo can be effectively used to classify abstracts for DTA systematic reviews. Further studies using other dataset are warranted to confirm our results. Additionally, we encourage the use of our framework and publicly available dataset for further exploration of more effective classifiers using other LLMs and prompts (https://github.com/youkiti/ARE/).	Kataoka, Y.; So, R.; Banno, M.; et al.	2023		10.1101/2023.10.31.23297818		(other folder)	Undermind	OASIS Lab	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.medrxiv.org/content/medrxiv/early/2023/10/31/2023.10.31.23297