AI for science, peer review and the scientific paper (new)	Discuss AI tools and the future of papers and peer review	N27		3 of 3	yes	1	The scientific paper needs an uncertainty layer	AI assistants and scientific agents increasingly shape how biologists navigate a literature no one can keep up with. They can extract results at scale, but judging what those results establish depends on how the evidence was generated, what alternatives remain, and how far the findings generalize. We argue that AI-ready papers should be machine-assessable, not only machine-readable. We propose an uncertainty layer that keeps these qualifications attached as scientific outputs move beyond the paper. By preserving the boundaries of what the evidence can support, this layer could help scientists use literature-scale AI for more reliable scientific judgment.	Rizzo, Kaeli; Weisman, Caroline M; Sever, Richard; et al.	2026	MetaArXiv		https://osf.io/preprints/metaarxiv/duymx_v1/	(other folder)		OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False	no DOI: not checked	True	False		Not in an ingest folder: found because it is close to the seed and Elicit papers	NEW	0.888	Peer review reports	0.867	NEW	0.811	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.806	NEW	0.689	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.681	0.796	OASIS Lab:8C2FPJXT
