Behavioural interventions and nudges (new)	Evaluate behavioural interventions and megastudies (out of scope)	N25		3 of 3	no	0	A guide to megastudies	How can behavioral insights best be leveraged to solve pressing policy challenges? Because research studies are typically designed to test the validity of a particular idea, surprisingly little is known about the relative efficacy of different approaches to changing behavior in any given policy context. We discuss megastudies as a research approach that can surmount this and other obstacles to developing optimal behaviorally informed policy interventions. We define a megastudy as “a massive field experiment in which many different treatments are tested synchronously in one large sample using a common, objectively measured outcome.” We summarize this apples-to-apples approach to research and lay out recommendations, limitations, and promising future directions for scholars who might want to conduct or evaluate megastudies.	Duckworth, Angela L; Milkman, Katherine L	2022	PNAS Nexus	10.1093/pnasnexus/pgac214	https://doi.org/10.1093/pnasnexus/pgac214	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://academic.oup.com/pnasnexus/article-pdf/1/5/pgac214/47812812/pgac214.pdf	False	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.726	Peer review reports	0.714	NEW	0.75	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.749	NEW	0.555	Peer review reports	0.55	0.724	OASIS Lab:CPML3M66
Behavioural interventions and nudges (new)	Evaluate behavioural interventions and megastudies (out of scope)	N25		3 of 3	no	0	A quantitative review of demand characteristics and their underlying mechanisms	Demand characteristics are a fundamental methodological concern in experimental psychology. Yet, little is known about the direction, magnitude, consistency, and mechanisms underlying their effects. We conducted a meta-analysis of 195 effect sizes from 40 studies that provided strict experimental tests of demand effects by manipulating the hypothesis communicated to participants. Results indicated that demand characteristics tend to produce small increases in hypothesis-consistent responding (d = 0.22, 95% CI [0.11, 0.33]). However, these effects were extremely heterogeneous (between-study τ = 0.30; within-study σ = 0.20), with the estimated distribution of true effects ranging from d = 1.97 (a large increase in hypothesis-consistent responding) to d = -1.44 (a large increase in hypothesis-inconsistent responding). Contra prior frameworks, we did not find evidence that demand effects were driven by participants’ motivation or opportunity to adjust their responses. We did, however, find robust evidence that such effects are driven by participants’ beliefs, as in placebo effects. Similar findings emerged in a direct replication of a study included in the meta-analysis. Taken together, results challenge conventional distinctions between demand characteristics and placebo effects. Furthermore, they underscore the importance of controlling for both response bias and placebo effects when estimating causal relationships.	Coles, Nicholas A.; Frank, Michael C.	2023	PsyArXiv	10.31234/osf.io/uw85a	https://psyarxiv.com/uw85a/	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://osf.io/uw85a/download	False	False			NEW	0.745	Causal inference	0.73	NEW	0.72	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.705	NEW	0.503	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.473	0.721	OASIS 