Bibliometrics, h-index and journal quality indicators (new)	Measure research output and journal quality (mostly 2023 Elicit results; probably out of scope)	N16		3 of 3	no	0	2023-Visualizing researchers’ scientific contributions with radar plot	2023-Visualizing researchers’ scientific contributions with radar plot — SoS Visualizing researchers’ scientific contributions with radar plot The essay advocates for diverse approaches in presenting a researcher's scientific contributions in a project. Taking inspiration from sports journalism and its visualization of football players' data, the essay suggests that a radar plot, incorporating CRediT contributor role data, enables multiple authors of a scientific paper to illustrate their contributions in a more specific manner. The suggested method, though subject to bias reporting, pays credit to different aspects of a research project, from conceptualization, analysis, administration, to writing and revising. It not only enables both academics and lay readers to better understand the considerable amount of work required in every project but also calls for the need to employ diverse viewpoints in science. Manh-Toan Ho is a researcher in the field of social sciences, with a background in media studies and economics. He is currently working towards his PhD, with a focus on using video games to address environmental issues in Vietnam. Manh-Toan has a broad range of interests, including video games, cinema, emotional artificial intelligence, and consciousness Toan would like those who appreciate this article to donate to Blue Dragon Children's Foundation ( Manh-Toan Ho (2023). Visualizing researchers’ scientific contributions with radar plot. Tran T, et al. (2020). Scrambling for higher metrics in the Journal Impact Factor bubble period: a real-world problem in science management and its implications. Problems and Perspectives in Management, 18(1), 48-56. Vuong QH. (2019). Breaking barriers in publishing demands a proactive atti