Biomedical research waste and cell-line problems (new)	Document irreproducibility and waste in biomedical research	N13		3 of 3	no	0	Some guidance on using mathematical notation in ecology	Mathematical modelling is playing an increasing role in studies of ecological systems. This requires the communication of the details of a mathematical model, including the use of mathematical notation to represent ecological variables, parameters and processes. In our experience, the clarity of mathematical notation varies between papers and can often be inconsistent with general conventions. Poor notation can impede communication and understanding of ideas, and make models appear more complicated than necessary. Here, we present some guidelines, including: (a) define every term in an equation, (b) use fonts appropriately (italicise mathematical symbols, use bold lower case for vectors and bold upper case for matrices), (c) use subscripts appropriately (to index quantities, for example, by year), (d) use superscripts appropriately (to indicate a power, the transpose of a matrix or the steady-state value of a quantity), (e) avoid multiletter variable names, and (f) revisit notation early on in a project to see if it should be refined. Although we focus mainly on ecology, our guidelines should be of interest to researchers applying models in evolutionary biology and broader areas of biology.	Edwards, Andrew M.; Auger-Méthé, Marie	2019	Methods in Ecology and Evolution	some guidance on using mathematical notation in ecology	https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/2041-210X.13105	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.719	Peer review reports	0.688	NEW	0.717	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.711	NEW	0.47	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.47	0.641	OASIS Lab:V5V6VTTA
