Biomedical research waste and cell-line problems (new)	Document irreproducibility and waste in biomedical research	N13		3 of 3	no	0	Engineering is evolution: a perspective on the biological design process	Careful consideration of the way we approach design is relevant to all fields of biotechnology. However, what we do when we design and how we should go about the process has received little thought in the context of biological engineering. To do so is crucial because existing paradigms for choosing and developing design methodologies may not yield the most effective design processes. Biology itself is capable of something like design: natural evolution is a powerful creative problem solver. We argue that design and evolution, in fact, follow the same cyclical process of generating variant solutions, testing these in an environment, and selecting the best performers. All design methods, including traditional design, directed evolution, and even random trial and error, exist on an evolutionary design spectrum, characterised by their population size and the number of design cycles. This is in contrast with conventional views that place directed evolution and rational design at odds with each other. Unifying design in this way provides a valuable conceptual framework for tailoring engineering approaches to challenging biological design problems.	Castle, Simeon D.; Gorochowski, Thomas E.	2023	OSF Preprints	10.31219/osf.io/urq9w	https://osf.io/urq9w/	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	False			NEW	0.755	Peer review reports	0.697	NEW	0.681	Assess novelty	0.673	NEW	0.47	Peer review reports	0.465	0.638	OASIS Lab:GJHXZ95W
