mong reviewers. Results demonstrated that collaboration improved agreement wit	Pier, Elizabeth L.	2017			https://www.proquest.com/docview/2133012434/abstract/F0CB0DDE89EF42F3PQ/1	Metascience	Metascience / MLAS-GPR	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False	no DOI: not checked	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.842	Peer review reports	0.826	NEW	0.833	Peer review reports	0.814	NEW	0.744	Peer review reports	0.674	0.818	OASIS Lab:5J44XDJA
Grant peer review and funding decisions (new)	Study how funders review proposals and allocate grants	N2		3 of 3	no	0	Improving the peer-review process for grant applications: Reliability, validity, bias, and generalizability	Peer review is a gatekeeper, the final arbiter of what is valued in academia, but it has been criticized in relation to traditional psychological research criteria of reliability, validity, generalizability, and potential biases. Despite a considerable literature, there is surprisingly little sound peer-review research examining these criteria or strategies for improving the process. This article summarizes the authors' research program with the Australian Research Council, which receives thousands of grant proposals from the social science, humanities, and science disciplines and reviews by assessors from all over the world. Using multilevel cross-classified models, the authors critically evaluated peer reviews of grant applications and potential biases associated with applicants, assessors, and their interaction (e.g., age, gender, university, academic rank, research team composition, nationality, experience). Peer reviews lacked reliability, but the only major systematic bias found involved the inflated, unreliable, and invalid ratings of assessors nominated by the applicants themselves. The authors propose a new approach, the reader system, which they evaluated with psychology and education grant proposals and found to be substantially more reliable and strategically advantageous than traditional peer reviews of grant applications. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)	Marsh, Herbert W.; Jayasinghe, Upali W.; Bond, Nigel W.	2008	American Psychologist	10.1037/0003-066x.63.3.160		Metascience	Metascience / MLAS-GPR	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		False	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.831	Peer review reports	0.814	NEW	0.828	Peer review reports	0.797	NEW	0.755	Peer review reports	0.69