Grant peer review and funding decisions (new)	Study how funders review proposals and allocate grants	N2		3 of 3	no	0	A Game Theoretic Approach to Peer Review of Grant Proposals	This paper studies the grant peer review process employed by the Turkish regional development agencies, which is adapted from a review procedure of the Education, Audiovisual and Culture Executive Agency of the European Union. To model this process, we consider a Bayesian strategic-form game played by three reviewers who observe both a common and a private score signal about an evaluated project and assign their scores to minimize the sum of their disutilities from the false acceptance and false rejection of the project. We numerically compute the Bayesian Nash equilibria of this game and conduct several comparative statics exercises, after calibrating the model parameters accordingly. We also introduce two simpler review processes and compare their performances to that of the calibrated process in terms of outcome statistics, involving pass and fail rates of the evaluated projects, and manipulation statistics, involving the reviewers’ manipulation rate and size of scores.	Bayindir, Esra Eren; Gurdal, Mehmet Yigit; Saglam, Ismail	2019	Journal of Informetrics	10.1016/j.joi.2019.100981	https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1751157719300355	Metascience	Metascience / MLAS-GPR	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		False	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.791	Peer review reports	0.761	NEW	0.782	Peer review reports	0.763	NEW	0.686	Peer review reports	0.632	0.769	OASIS Lab:K9AMLEQV
Grant peer review and funding decisions (new)	Study how funders review proposals and allocate grants	N2		3 of 3	no	0	The Selection of Scientific Talent in the Allocation of Research Grants	Career grants are an important instrument for selecting and stimulating the next generation of leading researchers. Earlier research has mainly focused on the relation between past performance and success. In this study we investigate how the selection process takes place. More specifically, we investigate which quality dimensions (of the proposal, of the researcher and societal relevance) dominate. We also study which phases in the process (peer review, committee review, interview) are dominant in the evaluation process. Finally, we investigate whether differences between disciplines are visible. The analysis of our data set, consisting of the reviews of 898 grant applications, shows that talent has different dimensions and therefore is not obvious. The evaluation of talent was found to be contextual, although there were only small differences between disciplines. Unlike the interviews with the applicants, the external peer reviews hardly influence the decision-making on grant allocation. The notion of talent was found to be the least evident in the social sciences and humanities.	van Arensbergen, Pleun; van den Besselaar, Peter	2012	Higher Education Policy	10.1057/hep.2012.15	https://doi.org/10.1057/hep.2012.15	Metascience	Metascience / MLAS-GPR	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.793	Peer review reports	0.78	NEW	0.762	Peer review reports	0.741	NEW	0.626	Peer review reports	0.575	0.769	OASIS Lab:X4S52BYA
