Grant peer review and funding decisions (new)	Study how funders review proposals and allocate grants	N2		3 of 3	no	0	How to Review Grant Applications	Why should you review grants? A cornerstone of any academic career is academic citizenship. This includes a broad range of activities from sustaining scientific integrity to participation in enterprises such as mentorship and manuscript and grant reviewing. Each year, hundreds of thousands of grant applications are received by the National Institutes of Health, Veterans Administration, NSF, foundations, state, and other granting agencies in the field of clinical and biomedical research. As a result there is a substantial need for experienced experts to review each of these grants. For new academic investigators, this potential opportunity raises a number of key issues. When is it appropriate for me to begin reviewing grants? Is there any benefit to my own career development by reviewing grants? How would I go about obtaining opportunities to review grants? Are there guidelines for how to best review a grant? The goal of this chapter is to address these key questions.	O’Hara, Ruth; Kesler, Shelli R.; Roberts, Laura Weiss	2020		10.1007/978-3-030-31957-1_40	https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-31957-1_40	Metascience	Metascience / MLAS-GPR	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.774	Peer review reports	0.761	NEW	0.773	Peer review reports	0.761	NEW	0.639	Peer review reports	0.572	0.765	OASIS Lab:BJASRVS7
