Grant peer review and funding decisions (new)	Study how funders review proposals and allocate grants	N2		3 of 3	no	0	Similar-to-me effects in the grant application process: Applicants, panellists, and the likelihood of obtaining funds	We analyse if and how the characteristics of grant research panels affect the applicants' likelihood of obtaining funding and, especially, if particular types of panels favour particular types of applicants. We use the UK's Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) award decisions to test the similar-to-me hypothesis for the first time in the grant context. Our main results indicate that panel members tend to favour more (or penalise less) applicants with similar characteristics to them, as the similar-to-me hypothesis suggests. We show, for instance, that the quality of the applicants is more critical for panels of high quality than for panels of relatively lower quality, that basic-oriented panels tend to penalise applied-oriented applicants, and that panels with fewer female members tend to penalise teams with more female applicants. As a whole, we show that similar-to-me effects are simultaneously at work for a wide variety of functional, job-related research characteristics as well as for more well-known demographic attributes.	Banal-Estañol, Albert; Liu, Qianshuo; Macho-Stadler, Inés; et al.	2023	R&D Management	10.1111/radm.12601	https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/radm.12601	Metascience	Metascience / MLAS-GPR	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1111/radm.12601	True	False			NEW	0.794	Peer review reports	0.777	NEW	0.755	Assess ethical matters	0.743	NEW	0.566	Peer review reports	0.534	0.754	OASIS Lab:TNFVZTRX
