HCI, LLM-as-a-judge and collective intelligence (new)	Study LLM judging, bias and human-AI interaction	N10		3 of 3	no	0	HARK No More: On the Preregistration of CHI Experiments	Experimental preregistration is required for publication in many scientific disciplines and venues. When experimental intentions are preregistered, reviewers and readers can be confident that experimental evidence in support of reported hypotheses is not the result of HARKing, which stands for Hypothesising After the Results are Known. We review the motivation and outcomes of experimental preregistration across a variety of disciplines, as well as previous work commenting on the role of evaluation in HCI research. We then discuss how experimental preregistration could be adapted to the distinctive characteristics of Human-Computer Interaction empirical research, to the betterment of the discipline.	Cockburn, Andy; Gutwin, Carl; Dix, Alan	2018	Proceedings of the 2018 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems	10.1145/3173574.3173715	https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3173574.3173715	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.801	Peer review reports	0.796	NEW	0.766	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.761	NEW	0.574	Peer review reports	0.568	0.736	OASIS Lab:DJYTB8VS
