HCI, LLM-as-a-judge and collective intelligence (new)	Study LLM judging, bias and human-AI interaction	N10		3 of 3	no	0	Improving the wisdom of crowds with analysis of variance of predictions of related outcomes	Decision-makers often collect and aggregate experts’ point predictions about continuous outcomes, such as stock returns or product sales. In this article, we model experts as Bayesian agents and show that means, including the (weighted) arithmetic mean, trimmed means, median, geometric mean, and essentially all other measures of central tendency, do not use all information in the predictions. Intuitively, they assume idiosyncratic differences to arise from error instead of private information and hence do not update the prior with all available information. Updating means in terms of unused information improves their expected accuracy but depends on the experts’ prior and information structure that cannot be estimated based on a single prediction per expert. In many applications, however, experts consider multiple stocks, products, or other related items at the same time. For such contexts, we introduce ANOVA updating – an unsupervised technique that updates means based on experts’ predictions of multiple outcomes from a common population. The technique is illustrated on several real-world datasets.	Satopää, Ville A.	2021	International Journal of Forecasting	10.1016/j.ijforecast.2021.03.011	https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0169207021000625	Metascience	JP - Personal | Metascience / JP-Personal	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0169207021000625	False	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.766	Causal inference	0.753	NEW	0.756	Peer review reports	0.755	NEW	0.522	Assess novelty	0.516	0.706	personal:KXBJKEJ4
HCI, LLM-as-a-judge and collective intelligence (new)	Study LLM judging, bias and human-AI interaction	N10		3 of 3	no	0	Fooled by beautiful data: Visualization aesthetics bias trust in science, news, and social media	Scientists, policymakers, and the public increasingly rely on data visualizations – such as COVID tracking charts, weather forecast maps, and political polling graphs – to inform important decisions. The aesthetic decisions of graph-makers may produce graphs of varying visual appeal, independent of data quality. While some have cautioned that graph beauty might bias how people interpret the data, it remains an empirical question whether the beauty of a graph influences how much people trust it. Here we comprehensively address this question in five pre-registered studies (N = 2,126). To maximize generalizability and external validity, we systematically sampled real-world graphs across three important domains: social media (439 graphs), news reports (110 graphs), and scientific publications (310 graphs). We consistently found that graph beauty predicted participants’ reported trust and the graph’s real-world popularity (Studies 1-3). Applying computational models, we showed that the graphs people perceived as beautiful were associated with vibrant colors and low-level features such as hue and luminance (Study 4). Based on these results, we manipulated graph beauty and demonstrated that beauty causally affected trust (Study 5). This causal effect was fully statistically mediated by people’s inferences about the general competence of the graph-maker (Study 5b). These findings reveal a novel source of bias in the interpretation of quantitative data and indicate the importance of promoting visualization literacy in this big data era.	Lin, Chujun; Thornton, Mark Allen	2021	PsyArXiv	10.31234/osf.io/dnr9s	https://psyarxiv.com/dnr9s/	Metascience	JP - Personal | Metascience | Metascience / JP-Personal	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://psyarxiv.c