Interdisciplinary science and knowledge synthesis (new)	Study how fields and scholars synthesize knowledge	N8		3 of 3	no	0	When is consensus knowledge based? Distinguishing shared knowledge from mere agreement	Scientific consensus is widely deferred to in public debates as a social indicator of the existence of knowledge. However, it is far from clear that such deference to consensus is always justified. The existence of agreement in a community of researchers is a contingent fact, and researchers may reach a consensus for all kinds of reasons, such as fighting a common foe or sharing a common bias. Scientific consensus, by itself, does not necessarily indicate the existence of shared knowledge among the members of the consensus community. I address the question of under what conditions it is likely that a consensus is in fact knowledge based. I argue that a consensus is likely to be knowledge based when knowledge is the best explanation of the consensus, and I identify three conditions—social calibration, apparent consilience of evidence, and social diversity, for knowledge being the best explanation of a consensus.	Miller, Boaz	2013	Synthese	10.1007/s11229-012-0225-5	https://doi.org/10.1007/s11229-012-0225-5	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://philpapers.org/archive/MILWIC.pdf	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.757	Peer review reports	0.75	NEW	0.735	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.709	NEW	0.532	Assess novelty	0.529	0.695	OASIS Lab:X99ZYM2F
