Journal peer review process and ethics (new)	Describe, critique or improve journal peer review	N4		3 of 3	yes	1	The limitations to our understanding of peer review	Peer review is embedded in the core of our knowledge generation systems, perceived as a method for establishing quality or scholarly legitimacy for research, while also often distributing academic prestige and standing on individuals. Despite its critical importance, it curiously remains poorly understood in a number of dimensions. In order to address this, we have analysed peer review to assess where the major gaps in our theoretical and empirical understanding of it lie. We identify core themes including editorial responsibility, the subjectivity and bias of reviewers, the function and quality of peer review, and the social and epistemic implications of peer review. The high-priority gaps are focused around increased accountability and justification in decision making processes for editors, and developing a deeper, empirical understanding of the social impact of peer review. Addressing this at the bare minimum will require the design of a consensus for a minimal set of standards for what constitutes peer review, and the development of a shared data infrastructure to support this. Such a field requires sustained funding and commitment from publishers and research funders, who both have a commitment to uphold the integrity of the published scholarly record. We use this to present a guide for the future of peer review, and the development of a new research discipline based on the study of peer review.	Tennant, Jonathan P.; Ross-Hellauer, Tony	2020	Research Integrity and Peer Review	10.1186/s41073-020-00092-1	https://doi.org/10.1186/s41073-020-00092-1	Metascience	INST 878 - Knowledge Infra / Final Paper INST 878 | Metascience | Preprint Lit	OASIS Lab, Open Science Readings, personal, preprint_literature	4	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://doi.org/10.1186/s41073-020-00092-1	True	True		Published 2022 or earlier: