Meta-analysis methods (new)	Describe and critique meta-analytic methods	N18		3 of 3	no	0	Comparison of three major meta-analytic approaches	Three major meta-analytic approaches have been developed and applied to integrate empirical research. A comparison of their statistical formulas suggested that they should yield different answers to the meta-analytic questions of central tendency, variability, and prediction by moderators. These frameworks were used to analyze systematically differing databases and showed that, although the techniques of L. V. Hedges and I. Olkin (1985) and of R. Rosenthal and D. Rubin (e.g., 1978, 1988; Rosenthal, 1991) tended to produce reasonable and convergent results, the results of J. E. Hunter, F. L. Schmidt, and G. B. Jackson (1982; Hunter & Schmidt, 1990) often diverged from the other 2 frameworks. For example, consistent with the law of large numbers, finding more studies with the same result is less likely to occur because of chance alone; the Hedges and Olkin and the Rosenthal and Rubin approaches confirmed this prediction, but the Hunter et al approach did not. The distinct tendency of the Hunter et al framework to produce results that violate conventional expectations suggests that it should be used with caution. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)	Johnson, Blair T.; Mullen, Brian; Salas, Eduardo	1995	Journal of Applied Psychology	10.1037/0021-9010.80.1.94		Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.728	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.704	NEW	0.778	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.749	NEW	0.581	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.526	0.724	OASIS Lab:S2WDQVGX
