Publishing, citations and research impact (new)	Explain or predict citation, impact and publishing practices	N5		3 of 3	no	0	Statistical relationships between corresponding authorship, international co-authorship and citation impact of national research systems	Abstract This paper presents a statistical analysis of the relationship between three science indicators applied in earlier bibliometric studies, namely research leadership based on corresponding authorship, international collaboration using international co-authorship data, and field-normalized citation impact. Indicators at the level of countries are extracted from the SIR database created by SCImago Research Group from publication records indexed for Elsevier’s Scopus. The relationship between authorship and citation-based indicators is found to be complex, as it reflects a country’s phase of scientific development and the coverage policy of the database. Moreover, one should distinguish a genuine leadership effect from a purely statistical effect due to fractional counting. Further analyses at the level of institutions and qualitative validation studies are recommended.	de Moya-Anegón, Félix; Guerrero-Bote, Vicente P.; López-Illescas, Carmen; Moed, Henk F.	2018	Journal of Informetrics	10.1016/j.joi.2018.10.004		SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.03366	False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.814	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.785	NEW	0.775	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.757	NEW	0.585	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.583	0.748	OASIS Lab:CI9SE4QH
