Publishing, citations and research impact (new)	Explain or predict citation, impact and publishing practices	N5		3 of 3	no	0	Twitter and the Spread of Academic Knowledge	A classic topic in the study of innovation is the link between physical proximity and the exchange of ideas. I’ve covered this on New Things Under the Sun, and one theme has been that scientists/inventors have various ways of keeping up with relevant new discoveries: they read journals, attend conferences, talk with people in their professional circle, etc. Especially with the advent of digital communication technology, none of that is particularly constrained by geography anymore - you don’t need to be close to where a discovery happens, in order to learn about it. While these methods are pretty good for keeping you up-to-date on things happening in your particular niche, they aren’t great for serendipity. If you don’t know that a field from outside your niche is relevant to your work, how do you know to read the journals, attend the conferences, or talk to people in that field? provide serendipitous encounters with new ideas is physical proximity: being around people who work on lots of different things means you can bump into people and learn what they’re working on, even if you wouldn’t normally expect their work to be relevant - but sometimes it is. in a relatively new kind of serendipity engine, which isn’t constrained by physical proximity: twitter (later renamed x, but forever twitter to me). Lots of academics use twitter to talk about new discoveries and research. Today I want to look at whether twitter serves as a novel kind of knowledge diffusion platform. We’ll mostly look at citations, an imperfect but widely available way of assessing the impact of a scientific paper. We could also look at article page views or downloads, but citations arguably provide a stronger signal both that an author learned about an idea and thought it was related to their work. Man					https://www.newthingsunde