Publishing, citations and research impact (new)	Explain or predict citation, impact and publishing practices	N5		3 of 3	no	0	What makes a productive Ph.D. student?	This paper investigates how the social environment to which a Ph.D. student is exposed during her training relates to her scientific productivity. We investigate how supervisor and peers' characteristics are associated with the student's publication quantity, quality, and co-authorship network size. Unique to our study, we cover the entire Ph.D. student population of a European country for all the STEM fields analyzing 77,143 students who graduated in France between 2000 and 2014. We find that having a productive, mid-career, low-experienced, female supervisor who benefits from a national grant is positively associated with the student's productivity. Furthermore, we find that having few productive freshman peers and at least one female peer is positively associated with the student's productivity. Interestingly, we find heterogeneity in our results when breaking down the student population by field of research.	Corsini, Alberto; Pezzoni, Michele; Visentin, Fabiana	2022	Research Policy	10.1016/j.respol.2022.104561	https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0048733322000853	Metascience	JP - Personal | Metascience | Metascience / JP-Personal	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0048733322000853	False	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.777	Peer review reports	0.766	NEW	0.731	Peer review reports	0.718	NEW	0.549	Peer review reports	0.514	0.697	personal:7TW249CZ
Publishing, citations and research impact (new)	Explain or predict citation, impact and publishing practices	N5		3 of 3	no	0	On whimsy, jokes, and beauty: can scientific writing be enjoyed?	While scientists are often exhorted to write better, it isn’t entirely obvious what “better” means. It’s uncontroversial that good scientific writing is clear, with the reader’s understanding as effortless as possible. Unsettled, and largely undiscussed, is the question of whether our goal of clarity precludes us from making our writing enjoyable by incorporating touches of whimsy, humanity, humour, and beauty. I offer examples of scientific writing that offers pleasure, drawing from ecology and evolution and from other natural sciences, and I argue that enjoyable writing can help recruit readers to a paper and retain them as they read. I document resistance to this idea in the scientific community, and consider the objections (well grounded and not) that may lie behind this resistance. I close by recommending that we include touches of whimsy and beauty in our own writing, and also that we work to encourage such touches in the writing of others.	Heard, Stephen B.	2014	Ideas in Ecology and Evolution		https://ojs.library.queensu.ca/index.php/IEE/article/view/5310	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False	no DOI: not checked	False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.821	Peer review reports	0.796	NEW	0.732	Peer review reports	0.72	NEW	0.526	Peer review reports	0.511	0.696	OASIS Lab:FXJXEJAC
