Reporting guidelines, preregistration and transparency (new)	Check or promote reporting completeness and preregistration	N17		3 of 3	yes	1	The past, present and future of Registered Reports	Registered Reports are a form of empirical publication in which study proposals are peer reviewed and pre-accepted before research is undertaken. By deciding which articles are published based on the question, theory and methods, Registered Reports offer a remedy for a range of reporting and publication biases. Here, we reflect on the history, progress and future prospects of the Registered Reports initiative and offer practical guidance for authors, reviewers and editors. We review early evidence that Registered Reports are working as intended, while at the same time acknowledging that they are not a universal solution for irreproducibility. We also consider how the policies and practices surrounding Registered Reports are changing, or must change in the future, to address limitations and adapt to new challenges. We conclude that Registered Reports are promoting reproducibility, transparency and self-correction across disciplines and may help reshape how society evaluates research and researchers.	Chambers, Christopher D.; Tzavella, Loukia	2022	Nature Human Behaviour	10.1038/s41562-021-01193-7	https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-021-01193-7	Metascience	JP - Personal | Metascience | Metascience / JP-Personal	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-021-01193-7.pdf	False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.818	Peer review reports	0.811	NEW	0.788	Peer review reports	0.778	NEW	0.639	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.628	0.796	personal:4EP6IPWB
