Reporting guidelines, preregistration and transparency (new)	Check or promote reporting completeness and preregistration	N17		3 of 3	no	0	A framework for assessing the trustworthiness of scientific research findings1	Within the research community, the question “what makes research findings trustworthy?” will elicit different answers depending on the emphasis on research integrity and ethics, research methods, transparency, inclusion, assessment and peer review, or scholarly communication. Each provides partial insight. We introduce a systems framework for conceptualizing trustworthiness of research findings to assess whether the research is accountable, evaluable, well-formulated, has been evaluated, controls for bias, reduces error, and well-calibrated with the claims matching the evidence. Each of these components has potential indicators of trustworthiness representing the research itself, the researchers conducting and evaluating the research, and the organizations supporting the research. The framework attempts to be applicable across methods, approaches, and epistemologies, but translation of principles into concrete, generalizable indicators is challenging. Developing valid, scalable indicators will improve research assessment, counter faulty trustworthiness heuristics, reduce misinformation, and ultimately foster earned trust in research.	Nosek, Brian A.; Allison, David B.; Jamieson, Kathleen Hall; et al.	2026	Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences	10.1073/pnas.2536736123	https://www.pnas.org/doi/full/10.1073/pnas.2536736123	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab, Open Science Readings	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.pnas.org/doi/pdf/10.1073/pnas.2536736123	True	False			NEW	0.831	Peer review reports	0.827	NEW	0.774	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.77	NEW	0.593	Peer review reports	0.582	0.758	OASIS Lab:CZWFH5UE
