Research misconduct, paper mills and retractions (new)	Detect or study misconduct, plagiarism and retractions	N14		3 of 3	no	0	Academic urban legends	Many of the messages presented in respectable scientific publications are, in fact, based on various forms of rumors. Some of these rumors appear so frequently, and in such complex, colorful, and entertaining ways that we can think of them as academic urban legends. The explanation for this phenomenon is usually that authors have lazily, sloppily, or fraudulently employed sources, and peer reviewers and editors have not discovered these weaknesses in the manuscripts during evaluation. To illustrate this phenomenon, I draw upon a remarkable case in which a decimal point error appears to have misled millions into believing that spinach is a good nutritional source of iron. Through this example, I demonstrate how an academic urban legend can be conceived and born, and can continue to grow and reproduce within academia and beyond.	Rekdal, Ole Bjørn	2014	Social Studies of Science	10.1177/0306312714535679	https://doi.org/10.1177/0306312714535679	Metascience	JP - Personal | Metascience / JP-Personal	OASIS Lab, Open Science Readings, personal	3	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://doi.org/10.1177/0306312714535679	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.766	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.734	NEW	0.785	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.735	NEW	0.608	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.545	0.735	personal:XTJMVBSB
