Scientific claim verification and text mining (new)	Extract, verify or retrieve claims from the literature with NLP	N11		2 of 3	yes	1	Echoes of Citations: Automated Extraction of Claims from Full Scientific Papers	Automated extraction of core scientific claims, concise statements of a paper’s primary contributions, is critical for navigating the growing scientific literature. We present a scalable framework that leverages citances, sentences from other papers citing the target work, as natural supervision, removing the need for costly manual labelling. Our method filters citances with a claim-focused rubric and aligns them with candidate claims to train two pipelines: an unsupervised extractor and a weakly supervised model. Experiments show our approach outperforms existing baselines, achieving up to 18% higher precision and 22% greater coverage. We further analyse claim distributions across paper sections and introduce a taxonomy of claim types, providing new insights into the rhetorical structure of scientific discourse.	Tan, Neşet Özkan; Tandon, Niket; Tafjord, Oyvind; et al.	2026	Proceedings of the AAAI Symposium Series	10.1609/aaaiss.v8i1.42589	https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI-SS/article/view/42589	(other folder)		OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI-SS/article/download/42589/50149	True	False		Not in an ingest folder: found because it is close to the seed and Elicit papers; Models split	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.937	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.91	NEW	0.842	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.794	NEW	0.699	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.649	0.833	OASIS Lab:W7H9TZTS
Scientific claim verification and text mining (new)	Extract, verify or retrieve claims from the literature with NLP	N11		2 of 3	yes	1	SciClaim2Explore: Claim-Guided Exploration of Scientific Data Evidence	Digital libraries provide access to research articles and related datasets and code, if available. However, even if a scientific statement is linked to supporting data, turning such relations into executable analyses requires separate workflows. This gap is increasingly urgent as computational reproducibility remains an imperative. Building on the TIB Knowledge Loom, which links scientific statements to supporting evidence, we present SciClaim2Explore, a system that turns statement-data links into entry points for natural-language exploration of research data supporting the claims conveyed by these statements. Starting from a selected scientific statement, in the proposed system a user asks a query about underlying linked, tabular data. The system uses the statement and schema context to generate and execute SQL, returns the resulting data table and, when appropriate, a data visualization. We evaluate the system on 84 claim-guided tasks from five scientific papers: 37 reproduce reported analytical results and 47 pose new analytical questions on the underlying data. In the grounding ablation, linked-dataset scoping reduces wrong-dataset queries from 14.3–28.6% to 0% and improves Answer Accuracy across three cloud models. These results suggest how scientific evidence links can provide executable context for exploring research data beyond artifact discovery. The source code is publicly available in the project’s repository1. A video demonstrating the system is also publicly available 2.	Ghaemi, Hadi; Alizadehsani, Zakieh; Stocker, Markus	2026	Proceedings of the 26th ACM/IEEE Joint Conference on Digital Libraries	10.1145/3805696.3846517	https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3805696.3846517	(other folder)		OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://doi.org/10.1145/3805696.3846