Social psychology, personality and scientific disagreement (new)	Report social and personality findings (mostly out of scope)	N20		3 of 3	no	0	Why Do Scientists Disagree?	Scientific research is often characterized by schools of thought. We investigate whether these divisions arise in part from differences in researchers’ cognitive traits such as their tolerance for ambiguity and the vividness of their visual imagery. These differences may guide researchers to prefer different types of problems, to tackle the same problems in different ways, and perhaps even reach different conclusions when studying the same problems in the same way. We surveyed 7973 researchers in psychology and allied disciplines and investigated links between what they research (area, topic, methodology), their stances on open questions in psychological science, and a set of validated scales assessing a variety of cognitive traits and dispositions. We also linked responses to publication histories from which we constructed landscapes of similarity and difference based on publication titles and abstracts, citations and co-authorship patterns. Our results show that researchers’ stances on scientific questions are, as expected, predictable from what kind of research they do. But they are also predictable from their cognitive traits, even when controlling for their research areas, methods and topics and co-authorship and citation patterns. These findings support the idea that divisions in scientific fields reflect not only differences in the kinds of data brought to bear on a topic, but also differences in the researchers themselves, hinting that the divisions may be more difficult to bridge than suggested by the traditional model of data-driven scientific consensus.	Sulik, Justin; Rim, Nakwon; Pontikes, Elizabeth; et al.	2023	PsyArXiv	10.31234/osf.io/9bd8k	https://psyarxiv.com/9bd8k/	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	False			NEW	0.782	Detect errors / limitations / qual