Social psychology, personality and scientific disagreement (new)	Report social and personality findings (mostly out of scope)	N20		3 of 3	no	0	Detecting Personality Traits Using Eye-Tracking Data	Personality is an established domain of research in psychology, and individual differences in various traits are linked to a variety of real-life outcomes and behaviours. Personality detection is an intricate task that typically requires humans to fill out lengthy questionnaires assessing specific personality traits. The outcomes of this, however, may be unreliable or biased if the respondents do not fully understand or are not willing to honestly answer the questions. To this end, we propose a framework for objective personality detection that leverages humans' physiological responses to external stimuli. We exemplify and evaluate the framework in a case study, where we expose subjects to affective image and video stimuli, and capture their physiological responses using a commercial-grade eye-tracking sensor. These responses are then processed and fed into a classifier capable of accurately predicting a range of personality traits. Our work yields notably high predictive accuracy, suggesting the applicability of the proposed framework for robust personality detection.	Berkovsky, Shlomo; Taib, Ronnie; Koprinska, Irena; et al.	2019	Proceedings of the 2019 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems	10.1145/3290605.3300451	https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3290605.3300451	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.76	Peer review reports	0.759	NEW	0.708	Peer review reports	0.705	NEW	0.528	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.526	0.704	OASIS Lab:I2EURJ2R
Social psychology, personality and scientific disagreement (new)	Report social and personality findings (mostly out of scope)	N20		3 of 3	no	0	Social evaluations of scientific occupations	Science and scientists are among the key drivers of societal progress and technological developments. While research has demonstrated that science is perceived as heterogeneous, work on perceptions of scientists usually considers “scientists” as members of a homogeneous group. In the present research, we went beyond this general categorization by investigating differences in social evaluations of different types of scientists. Across four studies conducted in the UK and the US (total N = 1441), we discovered that members of the most frequently mentioned scientific occupations (35 and 36 respectively in each country) are seen as highly competent, relatively moral, but only moderately sociable. We also found that individuals perceive differences between scientific occupations across social dimensions, which were captured in clusters of scientific occupations. Chemists, biologists, and physicists represented the most mentioned and highly prototypical scientific occupations. Perceived prototypicality was primarily associated with competence ratings, meaning that, in the public’s view, to be a scientist means to be competent. Perceptions of morality and sociability varied notably across clusters. Overall, we demonstrate that focusing only on “scientists” leads to overgeneralization, and that distinguishing between different types of scientists provides a much-needed nuanced picture of social evaluations of scientists across occupations.	Gligorić, Vukašin; van Kleef, Gerben A.; Rutjens, Bastiaan T.	2022	Scientific Reports	10.1038/s41598-022-23197-7	https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23197-7	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23197-7.pdf	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.794	P