Statistical errors and effect-size reporting (new)	Detect statistical anomalies and assess effect-size reporting	N7		3 of 3	yes	1	Including the Tool Statcheck in Peer Review to Increase Analytical Reproducibility: A Randomized Experiment in Two Psychology Journals - STAGE 1 REPORT	Many published papers in psychology and other fields are not reproducible: running the same analysis on the same data leads to different results. This undermines the credibility of reported findings. Some reproducibility problems, such as reported statistics that are internally inconsistent, can be spotted without access to raw data. In this study, we will test if using the tool statcheck in peer review could reduce such inconsistencies, by automatically flagging p-values that do not match their test statistic and degrees of freedom. We propose an experiment in two psychology journals in which, for the duration of one calendar year, a statcheck report will be included as an independent review in the editorial decision for half of the submissions that go out for peer review. We hypothesize that resulting published papers from the statcheck condition will contain fewer inconsistent p-values than papers from the control condition. We also hypothesize that there is a positive “spill-over” effect of the statcheck report and assess if overall reproducibility has improved more in the experimental condition than in the control condition. To test this, we will reanalyze the raw data of a subset of 20 papers per condition to verify all numbers in the Results section. Finally, we will keep track of rejection rates throughout the trial to assess if any reduction of statistical inconsistencies in published papers is due to corrections on the authors’ side or to a change in editorial rejection rates.	Nuijten, Michele B; van Ravenzwaaij, Don; Edlund, John	2026	MetaArXiv		https://osf.io/preprints/metaarxiv/gs4ft_v1/	SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False	no DOI: not checked	T