Systematic review methods and search strategies (new)	Explain how to search for and synthesize evidence	N6		3 of 3	no	0	Preferred reporting items for systematic review and meta-analysis protocols (PRISMA-P) 2015 statement	Systematic reviews should build on a protocol that describes the rationale, hypothesis, and planned methods of the review; few reviews report whether a protocol exists. Detailed, well-described protocols can facilitate the understanding and appraisal of the review methods, as well as the detection of modifications to methods and selective reporting in completed reviews. We describe the development of a reporting guideline, the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic reviews and Meta-Analyses for Protocols 2015 (PRISMA-P 2015). PRISMA-P consists of a 17-item checklist intended to facilitate the preparation and reporting of a robust protocol for the systematic review. Funders and those commissioning reviews might consider mandating the use of the checklist to facilitate the submission of relevant protocol information in funding applications. Similarly, peer reviewers and editors can use the guidance to gauge the completeness and transparency of a systematic review protocol submitted for publication in a journal or other medium.	Moher, David; Shamseer, Larissa; Clarke, Mike; Ghersi, Davina; Liberati, Alessandro; Petticrew, Mark; Shekelle, Paul G.; Stewart, Lesley A.	2015	Systematic reviews	10.1186/2046-4053-4-1		SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://systematicreviewsjournal.biomedcentral.com/counter/pdf/10.1186/2046-4053-4-1	False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.835	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.762	NEW	0.804	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.769	NEW	0.653	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.579	0.806	OASIS Lab:AQNRGUM2
