Systematic review methods and search strategies (new)	Explain how to search for and synthesize evidence	N6		3 of 3	no	0	Nuts and Bolts of Well-conducted Systematic Reviews: Research Quality Matters!	"James Lind conducted the first systematic review (SR) in 1753 and published a report that sought to offer a clear and objective presentation of the available data regarding scurvy.[1] However, the increasing need to enhance the state of evidence synthesis was not given significant emphasis until the 1970s and 1980s. ""Effectiveness and Efficiency: Random Reflections on Health Service,"" a textbook written by Archie Cochrane in 1972, highlighted the crucial nature of randomized control trials (RCTs) in figuring out the results of medical interventions.[2] Gradually, the need to enhance research synthesis was emphasized by policymakers, scholars, and clinicians around the world, that led to the formal establishment of SRs in the late 20th century in order to systematically summarize the available evidence to aid decision-making by various stakeholders and consumers.[3] REACHING THE ""BOTTOM LINE"" The plethora of ever-increasing medical literature makes it difficult, if not impossible, for busy clinicians and researchers to stay up-to-date. It also poses a number of technical and practical concerns about generalizing findings across the population, such as variations in the sample size, methods, assessment tools, representation of statistical test values, and missing outcomes, along with varying strengths and limitations of the conducted research. Murad et al. proposed a new evidence-based medicine pyramid wherein SRs and meta-analyses (MAs) are viewed as a ""lens"" through which the quantity and quality of the existing evidence is ascertained.[4] SRs are intended to yield robust, accurate, and reproducible conclusions, allowing for the review of observational, analytical, experimental (randomized or non-randomized) studies as well as case reports, case series, and qualitative "	Kulkarni, Ranganath R.; Tenagi, Saraswathi; Muliyala, Krishna Prasad; Desai, Geetha	2024	Journal of Psychiatry Spectrum	10.4103/jopsys.jopsys_47_24		SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://doi.org/10.4103/jopsys.jopsys_47_24	False	False			NEW	0.792	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.723	NEW	0.813	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.784	NEW	0.647	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.586	0.756	OASIS Lab:BW95HWHF
Systematic review methods and search strategies (new)	Explain how to search for and synthesize evidence	N6		3 of 3	no	0	Guidance on terminology, application, and reporting of citation searching: the TARCiS statement	<p>Evidence syntheses adhering to systematic literature searching techniques are a cornerstone of evidence based healthcare. Beyond term based searching in electronic databases, citation searching is a prevalent search technique to identify relevant sources of evidence. However, for decades, citation searching methodology and terminology has not been standardised. An evidence guided, four round Delphi consensus study was conducted with 27 international methodological experts in order to develop the Terminology, Application, and Reporting of Citation Searching (TARCiS) statement. TARCiS comprises 10 specific recommendations, each with a rationale and explanation on when and how to conduct and report citation searching in the context of systematic literature searches. The statement also presents four research priorities, and it is hoped that systematic review teams are encouraged to incorporate TARCiS into standardised workflows.</p>	Hirt, Julian; Nordhausen, Thomas; Fuerst, Thomas; et al.	2024	BMJ	10.1136/bmj-2023-078384	https://www.bmj.com/content/385/bmj-2023-078384	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.bmj.com/content/bmj/385/bmj-2023-078384.full.pdf	False	False			NEW	0.804	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.798	NEW	0.8	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.78	NEW	0.604	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.599	0.756	OASIS Lab:K53CDW48
Systematic review methods and search strategies (new)	Explain how to search for and synthesize evidence	N6		3 of 3	no	0	Subjective evidence evaluation survey for many-analysts studies	Many-analysts studies explore how well an empirical claim withstands plausible alternative analyses of the same dataset by multiple, independent analysis teams. Conclusions from these studies typically rely on a single outcome metric (e.g. effect size) provided by each analysis team. Although informative about the range of plausible effects in a dataset, a single effect size from each team does not provide a complete, nuanced understanding of how analysis choices are related to the outcome. We used the Delphi consensus technique with input from 37 experts to develop an 18-item subjective evidence evaluation survey (SEES) to evaluate how each analysis team views the methodological appropriateness of the research design and the strength of evidence for the hypothesis. We illustrate the usefulness of the SEES in providing richer evidence assessment with pilot data from a previous many-analysts study.	Sarafoglou, Alexandra; Hoogeveen, Suzanne; van den Bergh, Don; et al.	2024	Royal Society Open Science	10.1098/rsos.240125	https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11265885/	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab, Open Science Readings	3	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://royalsocietypublishing.org/rsos/article-pdf/doi/10.1098/rsos.240125/951538/rsos.240125.pdf	True	False			NEW	0.793	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.759	NEW	0.78	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.744	NEW	0.651	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.598	0.756	OASIS Lab:DRNVR5M9
Systematic review methods and search strategies (new)	Explain how to search for and synthesize evidence	N6		3 of 3	no	0	The influence of the team in conducting a systematic review	There is an increasing body of research documenting flaws in many published systematic reviews’ methodological and reporting conduct. When good systematic review practice is questioned, attention is rarely turned to the composition of the team that conducted the systematic review. This commentary highlights a number of relevant articles indicating how the composition of the review team could jeopardise the integrity of the systematic review study and its conclusions. Key biases require closer attention such as sponsorship bias and researcher allegiance, but there may also be less obvious affiliations in teams conducting secondary evidence-syntheses. The importance of transparency and disclosure are now firmly on the agenda for clinical trials and primary research, but the meta-biases that systematic reviews may be at risk from now require further scrutiny.	Uttley, Lesley; Montgomery, Paul	2017	Systematic reviews	10.1186/s13643-017-0548-x		SysRev-LLMApp pool	SysRev-LLMApp	OASIS Lab, personal	2	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://systematicreviewsjournal.biomedcentral.com/track/pdf/10.1186/s13643-017-0548-x	False	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.827	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.752	NEW	0.768	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.764	NEW	0.627	Peer review reports	0.621	0.755	OASIS Lab:KZV5SBHY
