Theory in psychology and philosophy of science (new)	Discuss theory, epistemology and the aims of science	N3		3 of 3	no	0	What Theory is Not	This essay describes differences between papers that contain some theory rather than no theory. There is little agreement about what constitutes strong versus weak theory in the social sciences, but there is more consensus that references, data, variables, diagrams, and hypotheses are not theory. Despite this consensus, however, authors routinely use these five elements in lieu of theory. We explain how each of these five elements can be confused with theory and how to avoid such confusion. By making this consensus explicit, we hope to help authors avoid some of the most common and easily averted problems that lead readers to view papers as having inadequate theory. We then discuss how journals might facilitate the publication of stronger theory. We suggest that if the field is serious about producing stronger theory, journals need to reconsider their empirical requirements. We argue that journals ought to be more receptive to papers that test part rather than all of a theory and use illustrative rather than definitive data.	Sutton, Robert I.; Staw, Barry M.	1995	Administrative Science Quarterly	10.2307/2393788	https://www.jstor.org/stable/2393788	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.757	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.751	NEW	0.729	Assess novelty	0.728	NEW	0.56	Assess novelty	0.541	0.721	OASIS Lab:Z68RVQ4T
