Theory in psychology and philosophy of science (new)	Discuss theory, epistemology and the aims of science	N3		3 of 3	no	0	Development of a formal system for representing behaviour-change theories	Use of natural language to represent behaviour-change theories has resulted in lack of clarity and consistency, hindering comparison, integration, development and use. This paper describes development of a formal system for representing behaviour-change theories that aims to improve clarity and consistency. A given theory is represented in terms of (1) its component constructs (for example, ‘self-efficacy’, ‘perceived threat’ or ‘subjective norm’), which are labelled and defined, and (2) relationships between pairs of constructs, which may be causal, structural or semantic. This formalism appears adequate to represent five commonly used theories (health belief model, information–motivation–behavioural skill model, social cognitive theory, theory of planned behaviour and the trans-theoretical model). Theory authors and experts judged that the system was able to capture the main propositions of the theories. Following this proof of concept, the next step is to assess how far the system can be applied to other theories of behaviour change.	West, Robert; Godinho, Cristina A.; Bohlen, Lauren Connell; et al.	2019	Nature Human Behaviour	10.1038/s41562-019-0561-2	https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-019-0561-2	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/10077414/3/West%20Theory%20specification%20paper%20Revised%20version%20in%20Nature%20format%20clean.pdf	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.761	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.748	NEW	0.776	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.761	NEW	0.557	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.525	0.718	OASIS Lab:58XU83CR
