Theory in psychology and philosophy of science (new)	Discuss theory, epistemology and the aims of science	N3		3 of 3	no	0	BLIND ANALYSIS IN NUCLEAR AND PARTICLE PHYSICS	▪ Abstract During the past decade, blind analysis has become a widely used tool in nuclear and particle physics measurements. A blind analysis avoids the possibility of experimenters biasing their result toward their own preconceptions by preventing them from knowing the answer until the analysis is complete. There is at least circumstantial evidence that such a bias has affected past measurements, and as experiments have become costlier and more difficult and hence harder to reproduce, the possibility of bias has become a more important issue than in the past. We describe here the motivations for performing a blind analysis, and give several modern examples of successful blind analysis strategies.	Klein, Joshua R.; Roodman, Aaron	2005	Annual Review of Nuclear and Particle Science	10.1146/annurev.nucl.55.090704.151521	https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.nucl.55.090704.151521	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/889148	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.701	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.686	NEW	0.751	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.748	NEW	0.506	Assess novelty	0.502	0.688	OASIS Lab:RGBDKXEF
Theory in psychology and philosophy of science (new)	Discuss theory, epistemology and the aims of science	N3		3 of 3	no	0	Ten Suggestions to Strengthen the Science of Ecology	There are few well-documented, general ecological principles that can be applied to pressing environmental issues. When they discuss them at all, ecologists often disagree about the relative importance of different aspects of the science’s original and still important issues. It may be that the sum of ecological science is not open to universal statements because of the wide range of organizational, spatial, and temporal phenomena, as well as the sheer number of possible interactions. We believe, however, that the search for general principles has been inadequate to establish the extent to which generalities are possible. We suggest that ecologists may need to reconsider how we view our science. This article lists 10 suggestions for ecology, recognizing the many impediments to finding generalizations in this field, imposed in part by the complexity of the subject and in part by limits to funding for the study of ecology.	Belovsky, Gary E.; Botkin, Daniel B.; Crowl, Todd A.; et al.	2004	BioScience	10.1641/0006-3568(2004)054[0345:tststs]2.0.co;2	https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article/54/4/345-351/284140	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		True	True		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.719	Peer review reports	0.709	NEW	0.729	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.713	NEW	0.515	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.475	0.687	OASIS Lab:VVSVKXY8
