Theory in psychology and philosophy of science (new)	Discuss theory, epistemology and the aims of science	N3		3 of 3	no	0	James' What Pragmatism Means	(1904), from series of eight lectures dedicated to the memory of John Stuart Mill, , in December 1904, from William James, Writings 1902-1920, The Library of America; Lecture II reproduced here; : by Andy Blunden in 1998 and proofed and corrected in March 2005. SOME YEARS AGO, being with a camping party in the mountains, I returned from a solitary ramble to find every one engaged in a ferocious metaphysical dispute. The corpus of the dispute was a squirrel – a live squirrel supposed to be clinging to one side of a tree-trunk; while over against the tree’s opposite side a human being was imagined to stand. This human witness tries to get sight of the squirrel by moving rapidly round the tree, but no matter how fast he goes, the squirrel moves as fast in the opposite direction, and always keeps the tree between himself and the man, so that never a glimpse of him is caught. The resultant metaphysical problem now is this: He goes round the tree, sure enough, and the squirrel is on the tree; but does he go round the squirrel? In the unlimited leisure of the wilderness, discussion had been worn threadbare. Every one had taken sides, and was obstinate; and the numbers on both sides were even. Each side, when I appeared therefore appealed to me to make it a majority. Mindful of the scholastic adage that whenever you meet a contradiction you must make a distinction, I immediately sought and found one, as follows: “Which party is right,” I said, “depends on what you practically mean by ‘going round’ the squirrel. If you mean passing from the north of him to the east, then to the south, then to the west, and then to the north of him again, obviously the man does go round him, for he occupies these successive positions. But if on the contrary you mean being first in front of him, t					https://www.marxists.org/reference/subject/philosophy/works/us/james.htm	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	HTML snapshot	False	no DOI: not checked	False	True			NEW	0.654	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.648	NEW	0.699	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.677	NEW	0.433	Check  reporting guideline compliance	0.408	0.609	OASIS Lab:DKDKGVGR
Theory in psychology and philosophy of science (new)	Discuss theory, epistemology and the aims of science	N3		3 of 3	no	0	Biology is more theoretical than physics	The word “theory” is used in at least two senses—to denote a body of widely accepted laws or principles, as in “Darwinian theory” or “quantum theory,” and to suggest a speculative hypothesis, often relying on mathematical analysis, that has not been experimentally confirmed. It is often said that there is no place for the second kind of theory in biology and that biology is not theoretical but based on interpretation of data. Here, ideas from a previous essay are expanded upon to suggest, to the contrary, that the second kind of theory has always played a critical role and that biology, therefore, is a good deal more theoretical than physics.	Gunawardena, Jeremy	2013	Molecular Biology of the Cell	10.1091/mbc.e12-03-0227	https://www.molbiolcell.org/doi/10.1091/mbc.E12-03-0227	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	True	https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3681688/pdf/	True	False		Published 2022 or earlier: check that it involves LLMs	NEW	0.675	Peer review reports	0.646	NEW	0.672	Detect errors / limitations / quality issues	0.666	NEW	0.455	Assess novelty	0.43	0.609	OASIS Lab:ALRYQ4S4
