Web pages, OSF projects, essays and commentary (new)	Index web resources and informal writing (little text to embed)	N19		3 of 3	no	0	Third Places and Neighborhood Entrepreneurship: Evidence from Starbucks Cafés	Sociologists have shown that “third places” such as neighborhood cafés help people maintain and use their network ties. Do they help local entrepreneurs, for whom networks are important? We examine whether the introduction of Starbucks cafés into U.S. neighborhoods with no coffee shops increased entrepreneurship. When compared to census tracts that were scheduled to receive a Starbucks but did not get one, tracts that received a Starbucks saw an increase in the number of startups of 9.1% to 18% (or 2.9 to 5.7 firms) per year, over the subsequent 7 years. A partnership between Starbucks and Magic Johnson focused on underprivileged neighborhoods produced larger effects. Several analyses suggest the effect occurs through a networks mechanism.	Choi, Jinkyong; Guzman, Jorge; Small, Mario L.	2024	National Bureau of Economic Research	10.3386/w32604	https://www.nber.org/papers/w32604	Metascience	Metascience	OASIS Lab	1	abstract (Zotero)	False		False	False			NEW	0.699	Peer review reports	0.69	NEW	0.68	Causal inference	0.675	NEW	0.381	Peer review reports	0.368	0.627	OASIS Lab:4YRSBXYT
